@lesson 06_linked_lists
@title Linked lists
@summary Nodes, function-based and class-based singly linked lists, Node**, deep copies and the doubly linked list idea.
@order 6

@section 6.1 What a linked list is, the node
@points
- A linked list is a chain of nodes allocated on the heap. Each node holds data and a pointer to the next node.
- The list is reached through a head pointer (the first node). An empty list has head == nullptr. The last node's next pointer is nullptr.
- Good: inserting and deleting does not shift other elements and the size can change at run time. Bad: no direct access by index (you must walk from the head).
- Every node is created with new, so every node must be deleted.
@code cpp
struct Node {
    int data;          // the stored value
    Node* nextPtr;     // pointer to the next node (nullptr at the end)
};

head -> [10|*] -> [20|*] -> [30|nullptr]

@section 6.2 Function-based singly linked list
@text
Operations:
@points
- InsertAtEnd: create the node; if the list is empty it becomes the head; otherwise walk to the last node and link the new node after it.
- PrintList: walk from head to nullptr printing data.
- DeleteWithValue: remove the first node holding a value. Cases: list empty; the head holds the value (move head forward); value found later (keep a prev pointer, set prev->nextPtr = cur->nextPtr, delete cur); value not found.
- DeleteList: walk the list and delete every node (save nextPtr before deleting the node).
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* nextPtr;
};

// Node** lets the function change the caller's head pointer
void InsertAtEnd(Node** ptrToHeadPtr, int newData) {
    Node* newNode = new Node();
    newNode->data = newData;
    newNode->nextPtr = nullptr;

    if (*ptrToHeadPtr == nullptr) {       // empty list: the new node becomes the head
        *ptrToHeadPtr = newNode;
        return;
    }

    Node* curNodePtr = *ptrToHeadPtr;
    while (curNodePtr->nextPtr != nullptr) {   // walk to the last node
        curNodePtr = curNodePtr->nextPtr;
    }
    curNodePtr->nextPtr = newNode;
}

void PrintList(Node* headPtr) {
    Node* curNodePtr = headPtr;
    while (curNodePtr != nullptr) {
        cout << curNodePtr->data << " ";
        curNodePtr = curNodePtr->nextPtr;
    }
    cout << endl;
}

// delete the first node that contains the given value
void DeleteWithValue(Node** ptrToHeadPtr, int value) {
    if (*ptrToHeadPtr == nullptr) {
        cout << "List is empty." << endl;
        return;
    }

    Node* curNodePtr = *ptrToHeadPtr;

    if (curNodePtr->data == value) {                 // the head holds the value
        *ptrToHeadPtr = curNodePtr->nextPtr;
        delete curNodePtr;
        return;
    }

    Node* prev = nullptr;                            // search, remembering the previous node
    while (curNodePtr != nullptr && curNodePtr->data != value) {
        prev = curNodePtr;
        curNodePtr = curNodePtr->nextPtr;
    }

    if (curNodePtr == nullptr) {
        cout << "Value not found in the list." << endl;
        return;
    }

    prev->nextPtr = curNodePtr->nextPtr;             // unlink, then free
    delete curNodePtr;
}

void DeleteList(Node** ptrToHeadPtr) {              // free every node
    Node* cur = *ptrToHeadPtr;
    while (cur != nullptr) {
        Node* next = cur->nextPtr;
        delete cur;
        cur = next;
    }
    *ptrToHeadPtr = nullptr;
}

int main() {
    Node* headPtr = nullptr;               // empty list

    InsertAtEnd(&headPtr, 10);
    InsertAtEnd(&headPtr, 20);
    InsertAtEnd(&headPtr, 30);
    cout << "Linked List: ";
    PrintList(headPtr);                    // 10 20 30

    DeleteWithValue(&headPtr, 20);
    PrintList(headPtr);                    // 10 30
    DeleteWithValue(&headPtr, 99);         // not found

    DeleteList(&headPtr);
    PrintList(headPtr);                    // (empty line)
    return 0;
}
@output 
Linked List: 10 20 30
10 30
Value not found in the list.

@section 6.3 Why Node** is needed
@points
- Parameters are copies. If a function receives Node* headPtr, it gets a copy of the head pointer, so assigning headPtr = newNode inside the function does not change the caller's head.
- To change the caller's head pointer, pass its ADDRESS: a Node** (pointer to pointer, see 02 topic 2.5).
- Inside the function:  *ptrToHeadPtr  is the caller's head pointer.
- At the call: InsertAtEnd(&headPtr, 10);
- PrintList only reads the list, so a plain Node* is enough.
@code text
void InsertAtEnd(Node** ptrToHeadPtr, int newData);   // can change head
void PrintList(Node* headPtr);                         // only reads
@mistakes
- Dereferencing curNodePtr when it is nullptr (check before using ->).
- Losing the rest of the list by overwriting a next pointer before saving it.
- Forgetting to delete nodes (leak) or using a node after deleting it.
- Forgetting the empty-list case.

@section 6.4 Class-based singly linked list
@points
- The same list as a class: Node has a constructor; LinkedList keeps head private and offers methods.
- The destructor calls clear() so all nodes are freed automatically when the list goes out of scope.
- Because the list owns heap memory it needs a copy constructor and an assignment operator that make a DEEP copy (new nodes with the same values); otherwise two lists would share nodes and delete them twice.
- operator= first checks this != &other (self-assignment), clears the old content, then copies.
- Methods that do not modify the list are marked const.
@code cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Node {
public:
    int data;
    Node* next;

    Node(int value) {
        data = value;
        next = nullptr;
    }
};

class LinkedList {
private:
    Node* head;

public:
    LinkedList() {
        head = nullptr;
    }

    // Destructor: free all nodes
    ~LinkedList() {
        clear();
    }

    // Copy constructor: build a separate list with the same values (deep copy)
    LinkedList(const LinkedList& other) {
        head = nullptr;
        Node* current = other.head;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            insertAtEnd(current->data);
            current = current->next;
        }
    }

    // Assignment operator
    LinkedList& operator=(const LinkedList& other) {
        if (this != &other) {            // protect against list = list
            clear();
            Node* current = other.head;
            while (current != nullptr) {
                insertAtEnd(current->data);
                current = current->next;
            }
        }
        return *this;
    }

    void insertAtEnd(int newData) {
        Node* newNode = new Node(newData);
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = newNode;
            return;
        }
        Node* current = head;
        while (current->next != nullptr) {
            current = current->next;
        }
        current->next = newNode;
    }

    void insertAtBeginning(int newData) {
        Node* newNode = new Node(newData);
        newNode->next = head;
        head = newNode;
    }

    // Delete the first node that contains the given value
    void deleteWithValue(int value) {
        if (head == nullptr) {
            cout << "List is empty." << endl;
            return;
        }

        if (head->data == value) {       // the head holds the value
            Node* temp = head;
            head = head->next;
            delete temp;
            return;
        }

        Node* current = head;
        Node* prev = nullptr;
        while (current != nullptr && current->data != value) {
            prev = current;
            current = current->next;
        }

        if (current == nullptr) {
            cout << "Value not found in the list." << endl;
            return;
        }

        prev->next = current->next;      // unlink, then free
        delete current;
    }

    void printList() const {
        Node* current = head;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            cout << current->data << " ";
            current = current->next;
        }
        cout << endl;
    }

    bool isEmpty() const {
        return head == nullptr;
    }

    void clear() {
        Node* current = head;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            Node* temp = current;
            current = current->next;
            delete temp;
        }
        head = nullptr;
    }

    int getSize() const {
        int count = 0;
        Node* current = head;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            count++;
            current = current->next;
        }
        return count;
    }

    bool contains(int value) const {
        Node* current = head;
        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->data == value) {
                return true;
            }
            current = current->next;
        }
        return false;
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;

    list.insertAtEnd(10);
    list.insertAtEnd(20);
    list.insertAtEnd(30);
    cout << "After inserting 10, 20, 30: ";
    list.printList();

    list.insertAtBeginning(5);
    cout << "After inserting 5 at beginning: ";
    list.printList();

    list.deleteWithValue(20);
    cout << "After deleting 20: ";
    list.printList();

    cout << "Trying to delete 100: ";
    list.deleteWithValue(100);

    cout << "Contains 10: " << (list.contains(10) ? "Yes" : "No") << endl;
    cout << "Contains 25: " << (list.contains(25) ? "Yes" : "No") << endl;
    cout << "Size: " << list.getSize() << endl;

    LinkedList list2 = list;             // copy constructor
    cout << "Copied list: ";
    list2.printList();

    list.clear();
    cout << "After clearing: ";
    list.printList();
    cout << "Is empty: " << (list.isEmpty() ? "Yes" : "No") << endl;

    LinkedList list3;
    list3 = list2;                       // assignment operator
    cout << "Assigned list: ";
    list3.printList();
    return 0;
}
@output 
After inserting 10, 20, 30: 10 20 30
After inserting 5 at beginning: 5 10 20 30
After deleting 20: 5 10 30
Trying to delete 100: Value not found in the list.
Contains 10: Yes
Contains 25: No
Size: 3
Copied list: 5 10 30
After clearing:
Is empty: Yes
Assigned list: 5 10 30

@section 6.5 Doubly linked list (idea)
@points
- Each node has two pointers: prevPointer and nextPointer. The list keeps head and tail pointers (and often a size counter).
- Inserting must update the neighbours in both directions, and the head/tail when inserting at an end.
- A complete DoublyLinkedList with InsertAtBeginning, InsertAtEnd and InsertAtPosition is given in Coding_Exam_2_Preparation.txt.
